# CakePHP Viator

Viator reads the CakePHP application and writes TypeScript for it. The PHP is the source of truth; the TypeScript is build output.

## Generated Files Are Output

Everything under the output directory (`resources/js` by default, overridable via `Viator.output`) is regenerated on every run. Never edit it, never patch around it, and never commit a fix to it — change the PHP that produced it and run `{{ assist.cakeCommand('viator routes') }}`.

```
{{ assist.cakeCommand('viator routes') }}                          # regenerate
{{ assist.cakeCommand('viator routes --path=resources/js') }}      # explicit output path
{{ assist.cakeCommand('viator routes --with-form') }}               # form helpers (on by default)
{{ assist.cakeCommand('viator routes --no-with-form') }}            # skip form variants
{{ assist.cakeCommand('viator routes --skip-types --skip-env --skip-broadcasts') }}
```

There is no analysis cache, so there is no `--fresh` equivalent: every run scans from scratch.

The same applies to types. Import `App.Model.Entity.User` from `viator/types` rather than writing a matching interface by hand; a hand-written copy stops matching the backend the first time a column changes, and nothing fails to tell you.

## What Generates What

| Output | Discovery |
|---|---|
| `actions/` functions | Scanned controller actions × `/{controller}/{action}/*` fallback plus explicit routes |
| `routes/` functions | `Router` named routes (`_name`; `/` and `:` become `.`) |
| Entity model types (`App.Model.Entity.*`, `MyPlugin.Model.Entity.*`) | Entity `@property` annotations (host plus `--plugin` / `Viator.scanPlugins` entity dirs, or `Viator.entityDirs`) |
| `Inertia.Pages.*` in `types.d.ts` | `$this->inertia('Comp', [...])` calls in controllers |
| `App.Form.*` in `types.d.ts` | `validationDefault()` validator DSL in `Form` classes |
| Enum unions in `types.d.ts` | Native `enum` declarations (backed values, pure cases; empty enums become `never`) |
| `vite-env.d.ts` | `import.meta.env.VITE_*` plus dotenv keys |
| `viator/broadcast-events.ts` | Classes exposing `broadcastEvent()` / `broadcastChannel()` / `broadcastData()` |

## Calling Routes

One action on one URL is one callable (`view({ id })`, `view.url(...)`). A param-less `index` action matches both `/{controller}` and `/{controller}/{action}/*`: the root URL keeps the plain name (`index()` → `/demo`) and the explicit one gets an `Explicit` suffix (`indexExplicit()` → `/demo/index`). Any other same-method collision keeps a URI-keyed dictionary (`shared['<key>'](...)`); when one URI answers to several verbs the keys are verb-prefixed (`handle['get /shared-uri/{name}']`).

A route function returns `{ url, method }`. `.url()` returns the string alone.

```typescript
view({ id: 1 });        // { url: "/demo/view/1", method: "get" }
view.url({ id: 1 });    // "/demo/view/1"
view.url(1);            // scalar shorthand also works

// Query parameters
view({ id: 1 }, { query: { page: 2 } });
view({ id: 1 }, { mergeQuery: { page: 3 } });
```

Method variants (`.get()`, `.patch()`) are there when a route answers to more than one verb. Spread `.form()` into an HTML `<form>`: it returns `{ action, method }` with `_method` spoofing already in the query string, because forms only speak GET and POST.

```typescript
deleteMethod.form.delete({ id: 1 });  // { action: "/demo/delete/1?_method=DELETE", method: "post" }
```

## Leaving Things Out

Viator can see more of the application than belongs in a browser bundle. Decide this deliberately for anything touching tokens, internal endpoints, admin-only relations or personal data.

Use the `#[ViatorIgnore]` attribute (`Crustum\Viator\Attribute\ViatorIgnore`) on a controller class or single action, or on an entity property. On a class nothing is generated at all; on an action it is dropped from `actions/` output (explicit named `routes/` entries are kept).

```php
use Crustum\Viator\Attribute\ViatorIgnore;

#[ViatorIgnore]
public function impersonate(): void
{
}
```

`unless: <condition>` keeps a declaration only while the condition holds; `when: <condition>` drops it while the condition holds. A condition is a `Configure` key or a `[class, method]` callable.

```php
#[ViatorIgnore(unless: 'Feature.enabled')]
public function beta(): void
{
}
```

Facts worth holding on to:

- Controller verbs come from the action body: `$this->request->allowMethod([...])` is restrictive (exactly those verbs), `$this->request->is('post')` checks are additive on top of GET.
- Request DTOs and container-injected services (class-typed parameters) never become URL segments; only builtin-typed and untyped parameters do. Parameters with defaults become optional (`{param?}`); route defaults also mark a parameter optional with its default.
- Vendor plugin controllers are NOT scanned by default; opt in per run with `--plugin` or via `Viator.scanPlugins`.
- Per-app trait helpers that are not actions go in `Viator.ignoreActions` (plain names, or `Controller::method` pairs), never in the generated output.

## Common Pitfalls

- Editing generated files. The next run overwrites the change.
- Redeclaring a model, page prop, form or enum type by hand instead of importing it from `viator/types`.
- Comparing against a string literal where an enum union exists. A renamed PHP case leaves the literal compiling and wrong.
- Assuming class-typed action parameters appear in the URL. They do not.
- Leaving a token, internal endpoint or admin-only relation in the generated output because nothing forced the decision.

## Verification

1. Confirm no file under the output directory has been edited by hand.
2. Confirm the frontend imports types from `viator/types` rather than declaring its own.
3. Confirm nothing sensitive appears in the generated output — search it for token, secret and internal-only field names.
4. Confirm `{{ assist.cakeCommand('viator routes') }}` runs clean and leaves no diff after a fresh run.
